#!/bin/sh
# PER-REPO pre-commit -- the STRICT gate, for a repo we own and have cleaned.
# (Enable with: git config core.hooksPath .githooks -- local config, so every clone must run the
#  installer once. CI does not depend on it.)
#
# Scope: the whole git-tracked tree, under the full synthetic-namespace rule -- nothing that merely
# LOOKS like a real-world identifier gets in, including a vendor nobody anticipated. That is stricter
# than the machine-wide global hook, which only checks the staged
# diff for breach classes because it also has to run in forks and research code without nagging.
#
# WHY THE OUTPUT IS CAPTURED INSTEAD OF PRINTED DIRECTLY (this bit is load-bearing):
# The obvious form is `"$PY" "$GUARD" --tree || exit 1`. It has a real hole, observed 2026-07-13:
# run `git commit | head -8`, let the guard print more than 8 lines, and `head` closes the pipe
# mid-write. The guard dies on the broken pipe, its exit status stops meaning what we think it means,
# and the commit sails through -- while the findings are still on screen, so it LOOKS like it blocked.
# A gate whose verdict the caller's pager can destroy is not a gate. So: capture the output, record
# the exit code with nothing in between, and decide from the code alone.
# --- IDENTITY ASSERTION (fail closed, no interpreter needed) ------------------------------------
# Is this commit going to be signed by the identity this repository's owner expects?
#
# Repeatedly, commits here have landed under an identity nobody chose: one form carried a GitHub
# noreply address with NO numeric account id, which links to no account at all, and took a history
# rewrite across a dozen repos to repair. Every occurrence had the same producer, and it was never
# the thing that got fixed: identity config quietly falling through to some default.
#
# So this check ignores how config resolved. It asks the repo where it belongs (origin), asks git
# who is about to sign (git var, which also sees GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL / GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL env
# overrides that no config rule can reach), and compares the two.
#
# pii_guard does not cover this and cannot: its author-email rule accepts ANY
# @users.noreply.github.com address, so a wrong-account address and the id-less form both pass it
# clean.
#
# THE EXPECTED-IDENTITY TABLE IS MACHINE-LOCAL ON PURPOSE: ~/.pii-guard/identities.conf.
# This file is vendored into public repositories, and an account map is exactly the kind of thing
# that should not be published as a side effect of shipping a hook. So the mapping lives outside
# every working tree.
#
# THE LOOKUP IS WHAT MUST FAIL CLOSED, NOT THE FILE PROBE. Until 2026-07-30 the "we degraded"
# announcement was keyed on whether the FILE existed, so the only failure it could describe was a
# machine with no table at all. Deleting or mangling ONE row -- the table has two, so one line does
# it -- left the file present, left _exp_email empty, dropped control into the weak address-shape
# branch, and passed a commit authored by the wrong account with rc=0 and NOT ONE LINE of output.
# Measured, not theorized. So every way the lookup can come back empty is now enumerated and each
# one is handled on purpose:
#
#   no origin remote              -> PASS with a note. There is no owner to have an expectation.
#   table file absent entirely    -> WARN loudly, shape check only.  (justified below)
#   table present, row malformed  -> BLOCK. A row that is not owner|name|email is never intentional.
#   table present, zero rows      -> BLOCK. A table that maps nothing is a disarmed gate.
#   row found but unreadable      -> BLOCK. Never infer "no expectation" from a failed read.
#   no row, but the committing identity is registered to a DIFFERENT owner
#                                 -> BLOCK. This is the incident: committing to X's repo as the Y
#                                    account. It fires whether X's row is missing, mangled, or fine.
#   no row for this owner, strict -> BLOCK, naming the owner and the file to add the row to.
#   no row for this owner, lenient-> shape check only, silently (see the policy note).
#
# WHY AN ABSENT TABLE WARNS INSTEAD OF BLOCKING. This hook is vendored into 18 PUBLIC repos. Any
# contributor who clones one and arms the hooks as CONTRIBUTING says has no ~/.pii-guard at all, and
# never will: it is the maintainer's private account map and must not be published. Blocking there
# turns the guard into a wall in front of every outside contributor, and a wall gets deleted -- which
# would also lose the address-shape check, the one part that needs no table and that catches the
# id-less noreply form that produced 17 unattributable commits. An absent table is also not the
# failure that has ever happened here; every real incident was on the maintainer's machine, where the
# table exists. So: absent warns, present-but-broken blocks. The loud warning is what keeps the
# degradation from being silent, which is the actual defect being fixed.
#
# NO ORIGIN -> NO EXPECTATION. A local-only repo has nothing to be checked against, so it passes
# with a note. That is not a hole: with user.useConfigOnly set, a repo no config rule maps still
# cannot commit at all, because git refuses to invent an identity rather than guessing one.
#
# ESCAPE HATCH, explicit and per repo, for a repo that deliberately commits as something other than
# its owner (a pseudonymous project, an org repo):
#   git config guard.expectedEmail <addr>
#   git config guard.expectedName  <name>
#
# THE CASE-FOLDER IS PROBED, NOT ASSUMED (same reason as the interpreter probe below).
# Every comparison here runs BOTH sides through the same fold. If `tr` is missing, each side becomes
# the empty string, "" equals "", no mismatch is ever recorded, and the whole assertion passes a
# wrong identity in silence -- measured, not theorized: with tr off PATH this hook exited 0 on the
# exact identity it otherwise blocks. A comparison whose failure mode is "everything matches" has to
# be proven able to run before it is trusted, so probe it once and fail closed.
#
# AND IT IS PROBED ONCE, NOT CALLED PER VALUE. `_lc` in a $(...) is a subshell plus an external
# `tr`, and on Windows a process spawn costs ~80 ms. The first version folded each value at its
# point of use -- nineteen spawns -- and MEASURED 1.7 s of wall clock on EVERY commit in EVERY repo
# on this machine, against 1.06 s for the whole hook before this block existed. A gate that taxes
# each commit by seconds is a gate that gets switched off, which is the exact failure this design
# keeps trying to avoid. So the probe stays and the folding is batched: one `tr` for all five
# committing values, and the expected values are read back out of the ALREADY-FOLDED copy of the
# table rather than folded a second time.
_lc() { printf '%s' "$1" | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z'; }
if [ "$(_lc ABC)" != "abc" ]; then
  echo "BLOCKED: the identity assertion cannot case-fold (\`tr\` is missing or not working)."
  echo "Without it every identity comparison silently succeeds, so the gate would pass anything."
  echo "Put a working coreutils \`tr\` on PATH (on Windows it ships with Git for Windows)."
  exit 1
fi

# POLICY for the one case the two hooks must answer differently: the table exists and names no row
# for THIS repo's owner.
#   strict  -- BLOCK. Set by the PER-REPO hook, which is only ever armed by someone who ran the
#              installer, and which only reaches this branch when a table EXISTS, i.e. on the
#              maintainer's machine. An outside contributor has no table and takes the absent path,
#              so strict never bites them.
#   lenient -- shape check only. Set by the MACHINE-WIDE hook, which also runs in ~50 clones of other
#              people's projects. Blocking every commit in every upstream clone is not a stricter
#              gate, it is a gate that gets switched off within a day. The cross-account rule above
#              still applies there, and it is the rule that catches the real incident.
_id_policy=strict

# Line $2 of the multi-line text $1, into $_row_out. Replaces a `sed -n Np` (plus the `tr -d` that
# had to re-strip its CRs), which is two process spawns for a job the shell can do with none. It
# also reads the SAME in-memory copy the scan above walked, so the row that matched and the row that
# gets read back can no longer come from two different reads of a file that changed in between.
# Sets a variable instead of printing, because $(...) is itself a fork and forks are what this is
# for. Returns nonzero when the line does not exist, and the caller must treat that as unreadable
# rather than as "no expectation" -- see the IDENTITY ROW UNREADABLE branch.
_row_out=""
_row_at() {
  _row_out=""
  _rn=0
  while IFS= read -r _rl; do
    _rn=$((_rn + 1))
    if [ "$_rn" -eq "$2" ]; then _row_out=$_rl; return 0; fi
  done <<_ROW_EOF
$1
_ROW_EOF
  return 1
}

_id_fail=0
_id_msg=""
_idfile="$HOME/.pii-guard/identities.conf"
_id_url=$(git remote get-url origin 2>/dev/null)
if [ -z "$_id_url" ]; then
  _id_msg="identity: no origin remote, so there is no owner to check against -- skipped."
else
  # Owner = the path segment before the repo name. Covers every remote form in use here:
  #   https://github.com/OWNER/repo.git      git@github.com:OWNER/repo.git
  #   git@<ssh-alias>:OWNER/repo.git         <ssh-alias>:OWNER/repo.git
  #   ssh://git@<ssh-alias>/OWNER/repo.git
  _p=${_id_url%.git}
  _p=${_p#*://}
  _p=${_p#*@}
  _p=${_p#*:}
  case "$_p" in
    */*) _owner=${_p%/*}; _owner=${_owner##*/} ;;
    *)   _owner="" ;;
  esac

  _aid=$(git var GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT 2>/dev/null)
  _cid=$(git var GIT_COMMITTER_IDENT 2>/dev/null)
  if [ -z "$_aid" ] || [ -z "$_cid" ]; then
    _id_fail=1
    _id_msg="IDENTITY UNRESOLVABLE -- git cannot name an author/committer for this repo.
  origin           : $_id_url
  owner (from URL) : ${_owner:-<could not parse>}
This is user.useConfigOnly doing its job: no config rule maps this repo to an identity, and git is
refusing to invent one from the OS. Set it explicitly:
  git config user.name  \"<name>\"
  git config user.email \"<id>+<login>@users.noreply.github.com\""
  else
    _an=${_aid%% <*}; _t=${_aid#*<}; _ae=${_t%%>*}
    _cn=${_cid%% <*}; _t=${_cid#*<}; _ce=${_t%%>*}

    # Every value this block compares, folded in ONE `tr` (see the note on `_lc`). Newline-separated
    # is safe here because git refuses to build an ident containing a newline, so no value can spill
    # into the next field; and if one ever did, the fields shift, the comparison MISMATCHES, and the
    # commit is blocked -- the failure direction this whole assertion is built around.
    _fold=$(printf '%s\n' "$_owner" "$_ae" "$_ce" "$_an" "$_cn" | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')
    { IFS= read -r _want
      IFS= read -r _lae
      IFS= read -r _lce
      IFS= read -r _lan
      IFS= read -r _lcn
    } <<_FOLD_EOF
$_fold
_FOLD_EOF

    # --- read the table ONCE, and record every way the read can go wrong -------------------------
    # The whole file is folded and stripped of CR in one pipeline instead of per row: a hand edit on
    # Windows turns this file CRLF, every field would then carry a trailing CR, every comparison
    # would fail to match, and the layer would disarm itself exactly the way a deleted row does.
    _exp_name=""; _exp_email=""
    _lexp_name=""; _lexp_email=""   # the same two, folded, for the comparison
    _tbl=0        # 1 = the table file exists
    _rows=0       # rows that parse as owner|name|email
    _badrows=""   # line numbers of rows that do not
    _hitline=0    # line number of the row for THIS owner
    _otherln=0    # line number of the row registering the committing address to a DIFFERENT owner
    if [ -f "$_idfile" ]; then
      _tbl=1
      # Kept in two forms of the SAME read: `_idraw` for anything quoted back to the operator (the
      # folded copy would print lowercased `git config` advice, which is wrong to paste), `_idlow`
      # for every comparison. Deriving `_idlow` from `_idraw` rather than re-reading the file also
      # means the expected identity can be lifted straight out of `_idlow` already folded, instead
      # of paying another `tr` to fold it back down.
      _idraw=$(tr -d '\r' < "$_idfile")
      _idlow=$(printf '%s\n' "$_idraw" | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')
      _ln=0
      while IFS='|' read -r _l _n _e _x; do
        _ln=$((_ln + 1))
        case "$_l" in ''|'#'*) continue ;; esac
        if [ -z "$_n" ] || [ -z "$_e" ] || [ -n "$_x" ]; then
          _badrows="${_badrows:+$_badrows, }$_ln"
          continue
        fi
        _rows=$((_rows + 1))
        if [ -n "$_want" ] && [ "$_l" = "$_want" ]; then
          [ "$_hitline" -eq 0 ] && _hitline=$_ln
        elif [ "$_e" = "$_lae" ] || [ "$_e" = "$_lce" ]; then
          [ "$_otherln" -eq 0 ] && _otherln=$_ln
        fi
      done <<_IDTABLE_EOF
$_idlow
_IDTABLE_EOF
      _other=""
      if [ "$_otherln" -gt 0 ]; then
        _row_at "$_idraw" "$_otherln"
        _other=${_row_out%%|*}
        # The rule below keys on _otherln, never on this string: a failed read must not be able to
        # turn "registered to somebody else" back into "nothing to say".
        [ -n "$_other" ] || _other="<the owner on line $_otherln>"
      fi
      if [ "$_hitline" -gt 0 ]; then
        # The matched row in ORIGINAL case, for advice the operator can paste.
        _row_at "$_idraw" "$_hitline"
        _row=$_row_out
        _exp_name=${_row#*|}; _exp_name=${_exp_name%%|*}
        _exp_email=${_row##*|}
        # ...and the same row already folded, so the comparison below needs no further `tr`.
        _row_at "$_idlow" "$_hitline"
        _lexp_name=${_row_out#*|}; _lexp_name=${_lexp_name%%|*}
        _lexp_email=${_row_out##*|}
      fi
    fi

    _ovr=0
    _o=$(git config --get guard.expectedEmail 2>/dev/null)
    [ -n "$_o" ] && { _exp_email=$_o; _ovr=1; }
    _o=$(git config --get guard.expectedName 2>/dev/null)
    [ -n "$_o" ] && { _exp_name=$_o; _ovr=1; }
    # A per-repo override did not come out of the folded table, so it is the one case that still has
    # to be folded here. One `tr` for both, and only on the rare path that declares an override.
    if [ "$_ovr" -eq 1 ]; then
      _fold=$(printf '%s\n' "$_exp_email" "$_exp_name" | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')
      { IFS= read -r _lexp_email
        IFS= read -r _lexp_name
      } <<_FOLD_EOF
$_fold
_FOLD_EOF
    fi

    if [ "$_tbl" -eq 1 ] && [ -n "$_badrows" ]; then
      _id_fail=1
      _id_msg="IDENTITY TABLE MALFORMED -- the owner-to-identity map cannot be trusted.
  table            : $_idfile
  bad line(s)      : $_badrows
Every non-comment line must be exactly  owner|name|email  with all three fields filled. A line that
is not is silently skipped by the lookup, which is how this gate got disarmed once already: with the
row gone the expectation is empty, the weak address-shape branch runs instead, and a wrong-account
commit exits 0 with no output. Repair the line, or comment it out with a leading #."
    elif [ "$_tbl" -eq 1 ] && [ "$_rows" -eq 0 ]; then
      _id_fail=1
      _id_msg="IDENTITY TABLE EMPTY -- $_idfile exists but maps no owner to any identity.
An empty table is not \"no expectation\", it is a gate with nothing in it. Add a row:
  <github-owner>|<name>|<account id>+<login>@users.noreply.github.com
or delete the file if this machine is genuinely not the maintainer's."
    elif [ "$_hitline" -gt 0 ] && { [ -z "$_exp_email" ] || [ -z "$_lexp_email" ]; }; then
      _id_fail=1
      _id_msg="IDENTITY ROW UNREADABLE -- line $_hitline of $_idfile matched owner '$_owner' during
the scan but could not be read back. Refusing to treat a failed read as \"this repo has no expected
identity\": that is precisely the silent degradation this check exists to prevent.
(Both the original-case and the folded copy of that line are read back; this fires if either is
empty, so a comparison can never run against half a row.)"
    elif [ -n "$_exp_email" ]; then
      _bad=""
      [ "$_lae" = "$_lexp_email" ] || _bad="author email"
      [ "$_lce" = "$_lexp_email" ] || _bad="${_bad:+$_bad, }committer email"
      if [ -n "$_exp_name" ]; then
        [ "$_lan" = "$_lexp_name" ] || _bad="${_bad:+$_bad, }author name"
        [ "$_lcn" = "$_lexp_name" ] || _bad="${_bad:+$_bad, }committer name"
      fi
      if [ -n "$_bad" ]; then
        _id_fail=1
        _id_msg="IDENTITY MISMATCH -- this commit would be attributed to the wrong account.
  origin           : $_id_url
  owner (from URL) : $_owner
  expected         : $_exp_name <$_exp_email>
  author           : $_an <$_ae>
  committer        : $_cn <$_ce>
  mismatched       : $_bad
Fix it repo-locally, which wins over every global rule:
  git config user.name  \"$_exp_name\"
  git config user.email \"$_exp_email\"
If this repo commits under a different identity ON PURPOSE, declare that instead of loosening this:
  git config guard.expectedEmail <addr>   (and guard.expectedName)"
      fi
    elif [ "$_otherln" -gt 0 ]; then
      # No row for this owner, but the address about to sign IS one of the registered ones -- filed
      # under somebody else. That is the cross-account commit this whole assertion was built for, and
      # it is reachable ONLY because the row that would have caught it is missing or malformed. Never
      # let it through on the strength of the lookup that came back empty.
      #
      # This blocks under BOTH policies, including in a clone of somebody else's project, and that is
      # deliberate: the operator is committing AS THEMSELVES there, so "no expectation exists" is
      # false -- one does, the table just cannot state it. Unlike the absent-table case there is a
      # one-line fix and the message leads with it, so this is a gate that can be satisfied, not a
      # wall. (The alternative, inferring "which owners are really ours", is circular: the only
      # record of that is the row that went missing.)
      _id_fail=1
      _id_msg="IDENTITY CROSS-ACCOUNT -- committing to one owner's repo as another owner's account.
Declare which it should be, once, then this never asks again:
  add a row to $_idfile
      ${_owner:-<owner>}|<name>|<account id>+<login>@users.noreply.github.com
  or, for this clone only:
      git config guard.expectedEmail <addr>   (and guard.expectedName)

  origin           : $_id_url
  owner (from URL) : ${_owner:-<could not parse>}
  author           : $_an <$_ae>
  committer        : $_cn <$_ce>
  that address is registered on line $_otherln of the table, to owner: $_other
  and there is NO row in that table for owner: ${_owner:-<could not parse>}"
    elif [ "$_tbl" -eq 1 ] && [ -n "$_owner" ] && [ "$_id_policy" = strict ]; then
      _id_fail=1
      _id_msg="IDENTITY OWNER NOT IN TABLE -- no expected identity could be resolved for this repo.
  origin           : $_id_url
  owner (from URL) : $_owner
  table            : $_idfile   ($_rows row(s) parsed, none for '$_owner')
  author           : $_an <$_ae>
  committer        : $_cn <$_ce>
This hook is the STRICT per-repo gate, so an unresolvable expectation is a block, not a shrug: an
empty lookup used to fall through to the address-shape check and pass a wrong-account commit in
silence. Do one of these, on purpose:
  add the row      -> $_owner|<name>|<account id>+<login>@users.noreply.github.com
  or declare it    -> git config guard.expectedEmail <addr>   (and guard.expectedName)"
    else
      # Nothing in the table speaks to this commit. Fall back to the one invariant that needs no
      # table: a GitHub noreply address must carry the numeric account id. The id-less
      # <login>@users.noreply.github.com form is the shape that produced 17 unattributable commits,
      # and it is indistinguishable from a correct address to anything that only checks the domain.
      _shape_bad=""
      for _pair in "author:$_ae" "committer:$_ce"; do
        _who=${_pair%%:*}
        _addr=${_pair#*:}
        case "$_addr" in
          *@users.noreply.github.com)
            _lp=${_addr%@users.noreply.github.com}
            case "$_lp" in
              *+*) _num=${_lp%%+*}
                   case "$_num" in ''|*[!0-9]*) _shape_bad="${_shape_bad:+$_shape_bad, }$_who" ;; esac ;;
              *)   _shape_bad="${_shape_bad:+$_shape_bad, }$_who" ;;
            esac ;;
        esac
      done
      if [ -n "$_shape_bad" ]; then
        _id_fail=1
        _id_msg="IDENTITY MALFORMED -- a GitHub noreply address with no numeric account id.
  origin           : $_id_url
  owner (from URL) : ${_owner:-<could not parse>}
  author           : $_an <$_ae>
  committer        : $_cn <$_ce>
  malformed        : $_shape_bad
<login>@users.noreply.github.com is attributed to NO account. The linkable form is
<account id>+<login>@users.noreply.github.com (find the id with: gh api users/<login> --jq .id)."
      elif [ "$_tbl" -eq 0 ]; then
        # Say this out loud. A missing table means the gate dropped to its weak form, and silent
        # degradation is the failure mode this whole guard exists to stop.
        _id_msg="identity: no $_idfile on this machine, so only the address-shape check ran.
Owner-to-identity matching is OFF until that table exists. If this is the maintainer's machine, that
is a disarmed gate, not a clean run."
      elif [ -z "$_owner" ]; then
        # Table present but the remote is a form with no owner segment (a filesystem remote, say).
        # Legitimate, and not something to block on, but it must not look like a checked commit.
        _id_msg="identity: origin '$_id_url' has no owner segment to look up, so only the
address-shape check ran."
      fi
      # Table present, owner simply is not one of ours, and nothing in the table knows this address
      # either: a third party's repo. There is genuinely no expectation to state, so say nothing.
      # One informational line per commit across ~50 upstream clones is exactly the nagging that
      # gets a gate switched off.
    fi
  fi
fi
if [ "$_id_fail" -ne 0 ]; then
  printf '%s\n' "$_id_msg"
  echo ""
  echo "commit BLOCKED by the identity assertion. Do not --no-verify: an author line is baked into"
  echo "the commit object, so after this the fix is a history rewrite and a force push, on every"
  echo "clone and every tag that pins the old commit."
  exit 1
fi
# A full strict match is silent -- a gate that talks on every successful commit gets tuned out. The
# only thing worth saying is that the check could NOT be made in full, which is what _id_msg holds.
if [ -n "$_id_msg" ]; then
  printf '%s\n' "$_id_msg"
fi
# --- end identity assertion ---------------------------------------------------------------------

ROOT="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
GUARD="$ROOT/tools/pii_guard.py"
BOUNDARY="$ROOT/tools/data_boundary.py"
# FAIL CLOSED on a missing guard, exactly as .githooks/pre-push already does for the same condition.
# This line used to read `[ -f "$GUARD" ] || exit 0` with the comment "guard not vendored into this
# repo yet: do not block". That state cannot occur: this hook and tools/pii_guard.py are vendored by
# the same installer in the same operation, so a repo running THIS FILE is by definition a repo that
# was vendored. Checked across the fleet: every repo without the guard is also without this hook.
# So a missing guard here has exactly one meaning, someone deleted it, and until now that silently
# switched commit protection off while printing nothing. pre-push proved the shape on 2026-08-30 by
# removing the scanner and watching a push carrying an address exit 0 in silence. Two hooks in one
# repo were answering the same question in opposite ways, and the quiet answer sat on the block path.
if [ ! -f "$GUARD" ]; then
  echo "BLOCKED: $GUARD is missing, so nothing is checking this commit."
  echo "Restore it with the pii-guard installer rather than committing blind."
  exit 1
fi
# A MISSING INTERPRETER MUST FAIL CLOSED. This used to be
#   PY=$(command -v python || command -v python3 || command -v py) || exit 0
# which means: on any machine where python does not resolve, every gate below silently does nothing
# and the operation sails through looking guarded. That is not hypothetical here -- a Windows PATH
# that resolved `python` only to the WindowsApps App Execution Alias has already burned this
# operator, and a stub RESOLVES: `command -v` finds it, it just cannot run anything. So probe the
# interpreter, never infer from the fact that a name resolved.
PY=""
for _cand in python python3 py; do
  _bin=$(command -v "$_cand" 2>/dev/null) || continue
  "$_bin" -c "import sys" >/dev/null 2>&1 || continue
  PY=$_bin
  break
done
if [ -z "$PY" ]; then
  echo "BLOCKED: no WORKING python interpreter found (tried python, python3, py)."
  echo "The guards cannot run, so nothing at all would be checking this."
  echo "On Windows this is usually PATH resolving only the WindowsApps stub, which resolves but"
  echo "cannot execute. Install a real python or put it ahead of WindowsApps on PATH."
  exit 1
fi

# The guard's output is printed on EVERY run, not only on failure. See the same note in
# global-hooks/pre-commit: HISTORY-DEBT lines, WARN lines and the exemption receipt are all
# things the guard says while exiting 0, and a hook that speaks only on nonzero deletes them.
out=$("$PY" "$GUARD" --tree 2>&1)
rc=$?
[ -n "$out" ] && printf '%s\n' "$out"
if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
  echo ""
  echo "pre-commit BLOCKED by pii_guard. Do not reach for --no-verify:"
  echo "right now the fix is an edit; after this commit it is a history rewrite."
  exit 1
fi

# The DATA BOUNDARY -- the primary control, of which pii_guard is only the backstop.
#
# pii_guard reads what you are about to publish and looks for things that SMELL private. That
# accepts the premise that real data is flowing toward the exit at all, and it is why the 2026-07
# audit found real-run output from a private account in public repos: a ticker with
# an entry price contains no email, no phone, no ZIP. Nothing to smell. The scanner was clean and
# the repo was full of somebody's life.
#
# So this check asks the structural question instead: is any DATA-class path in the index? A public
# skill repo is an UNINITIALIZED TOOL -- real-run output lives in the private companion config, and
# an agent writing this repo has nothing real within reach to copy. No manifest -> exits 0, so this
# is a no-op in repos that have not declared their classes yet.
if [ ! -f "$BOUNDARY" ]; then
  echo "BLOCKED: $BOUNDARY is missing, so the data boundary is unchecked."
